00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.140
HELLO INTERNET!

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.200
Welcome to Film Theory,

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.020
where we don't shy away from the scariest corners of the Internet.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.600
And I'm not talking about Dark Web or Russian hackers,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.480
I'm talking about ONLINE REVIEWS!

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.400
Now, if you've made any YouTube videos of your own,

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.280
you know the scariest part of being online

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.220
is what everyone else THINKS about you being online.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:00.060
In a place where everyone is an influencer in some way and can review anything at anytime,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.640
there's some serious stress when it comes to putting yourself out there to be judged.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.080
I mean, just look at me.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.500
To a lot of people, I am the LITERAL cancer of the Internet that they just can't chemotherapy away.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.100
Sorry guys, I can't help it if people take my theories just a *wee bit* too seriously.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.040
But YouTubers are NOTHING compared to the paranoia of big movie studios

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.320
Where the biggest companies on the planet spend the biggest dollar amounts on the planet

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.040
to make sure you're getting the best impression of their movie.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.200
Whether it's through cool marketing gimmicks like recreating the entirety of Westworld,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.060
or just telling you that Ebert and Robert gave it 35 thumbs up!

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.840
Movie reviews that come out before anyone has had a chance to see a movie

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.660
can mean the difference of millions of dollars at the box office.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.940
Just look at one of the best examples from last year: Justice League.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.240
One of the most intensely judged, and also least-watched movies of 2017.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.140
Critics hated it, fans were divided about it, and

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.240
by the time it came out almost no one went to see it.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.640
Even more bizarrely though, for a movie that didn't have itself a lot of viewers,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.440
it certainly caused an all-out Internet war on places like Rotten Tomatoes:

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.280
a site that is KNOWN for tanking box office sales if it puts out a bad review score.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.300
Things got so heated that it wasn't all that surprising when

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.060
accusations of "review rigging" started to pop up.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.960
Claiming that the reviews weren't from real moviegoers but were instead from, the scourge of the Internet,

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.160
Bot Accounts.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.540
Seriously, accusations surfaced all across the Internet on both sides of the aisle,

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:29.200
with one side claiming that Marvel and Disney unleashed bots with Marvel bias to tank the performance of "Justice League"

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.900
and on the other side, there were accusations that DC had to create fake bot reivews

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.820
so they could still claim that people liked the movie in the first place!

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.500
And honestly, if you stop and think about it, can you blame any of this suspicion?

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.460
Take one look at the reviews on Rotten Tomatoes for "Justice League"

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.900
and it definitely seems like something fishy's going on.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.940
An 80% Audience Approval Rating?

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.620
I gotta say, that made me do a little bit of a double-take,

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.260
especially when you compare it to the critic's score of 41 percent!

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:59.160
Did audiences really like it more than TWICE as much as critics?

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.260
I mean, I saw the movie and it was... fine.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.100
It wasn't as high as an 80% but it definitely wasn't as low as a 40%.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.300
It was... okay.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:12.480
But could DC be using bots to up its score to ensure that America saw the Red-Orange fight scenes that they had worked so hard on?

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.680
Is it really that far-fetched of a claim?

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:18.440
These movies cost hundreds of millions of dollars to make,

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:23.780
so wouldn't it make sense for the big studios to spend just a little bit more to nudge the odds ever in their favor?

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.580
So that, ladies and gentlemen, is the episode of today:

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:32.220
Explore the world of bots in order to determine whether the reviews of "Justice League" were influenced,

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.040
either for the good or the bad, by a bot army attack.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:41.120
Don't just believe the speculative headlines, today the TRUTH shall be revealed.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.060
Every day, more and more stories seem to start circulating about bots.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.480
Bots rigging elections,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.700
Yelp bots rigging reviews of restaurants,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.760
But to understand how bot systems work

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.400
and to figure out whether it's possible for movie studios to rig a system like Rotten Tomatoes,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.040
we first have to have a solid understanding of what bots actually are.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.540
The term gets thrown around quite a bit,

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.040
but do you actually know what a bot IS?

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.180
Before researching this episode I thought I did,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.240
but the world of Internet botting is a much deeper rabbit hole than I first realized.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:16.680
Web Robots or "Bots" for short are basically an online program that can do a whole bunch of little tasks automatically

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.800
without anyone actually having to control what's going on from a computer.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.380
In theory, they're awesome.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.300
They're basically the worker BEES of the Internet

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.480
that can complete the same task over and over and over again

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.560
without a human having to waste the time to do it.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.020
And in a lot of cases, they are a good thing.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:35.360
There are news stories coming out about bots being able to make people's jobs easier.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.180
By answering tech support questions without keeping customers on hold for hours

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.520
to stopping streams of negativity and harassment on social media.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:48.260
75% of executives at the world's biggest companies say that using bots for things like talking to customers

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.500
isn't just an "if", it's a "when".

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.880
So then, what's the big problem here?

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.120
Well, the issue is that the technology is getting SO good that you can't tell a bot from a person.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.260
Which makes it almost impossible to trust anything that you see said on the Internet.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.900
To show you what I mean, let's take a look at one type of bot.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.220
The IRC Bot.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.840
The IRC (or Internet Relay Chat Bot)

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:09.340
Can be designed to look like a person on any website that has a chat function *hi*

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:09.840
idk

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.600
 

